          Unlike low-frequency ultrasound (20–40 kHz) which relies on destructive transient cavitation bubble implosion, high-frequency megasonic waves (0.5–3.0 MHz) operate in the acoustic micro-streaming regime. Boundary-layer acoustic attenuation generates viscous Eckart bulk streaming and localized Rayleigh micro-eddies:
        </p>
